Presidential elections were held in France on 23 April and 7 May 2017. Incumbent president [[François Hollande]] of the [[Socialist Party (France)|Socialist Party]] (PS) was eligible to run for a second term, but declared on 1 December 2016 that he would not seek reelection in light of low approval ratings, making him the first incumbent head of state of the [[French Fifth Republic|Fifth Republic]] not to seek reelection. As no candidate won a majority in the first round, a [[Two-round system|runoff]] was held between the top two candidates, [[Emmanuel Macron]] of [[La République En Marche!|En Marche!]] (EM) and [[Marine Le Pen]] of the [[National Rally (France)|National Front]] (FN), which Macron won with a difference of more than 30% of the vote. 

[[François Fillon]] of [[The Republicans (France)|The Republicans]] (LR)—after winning the party's [[2016 The Republicans (France) presidential primary|first open primary]]—and Le Pen of the National Front led first-round [[Opinion polling for the 2017 French presidential election|opinion polls]] in November 2016 and mid-January 2017. Polls tightened considerably by late January; after the publication of revelations that Fillon employed family members in possibly fictitious jobs in a series of politico-financial affairs that came to be colloquially known as "[[Fillon affair|Penelopegate]]", Macron overtook Fillon to place consistently second in first-round polling. At the same time, [[Benoît Hamon]] won the [[2017 French Socialist Party presidential primary|Socialist primary]], entering fourth place in the polls. After strong debate performances, [[Jean-Luc Mélenchon]] of [[La France Insoumise]] (FI) rose significantly in polls in late March, overtaking Hamon to place just below Fillon.

The first round was held under a state of emergency that was declared following the [[November 2015 Paris attacks]].<ref>{{cite news|title=La France aux urnes pour le premier tour de la présidentielle 2017|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/04/23/la-france-aux-urnes-pour-le-premier-tour-de-la-presidentielle-2017_5115733_4854003.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=23 April 2017|access-date=28 April 2017|archive-date=7 July 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220707091842/https://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/04/23/la-france-aux-urnes-pour-le-premier-tour-de-la-presidentielle-2017_5115733_4854003.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Following the result of the first round, Macron and Le Pen continued to the 7 May runoff.<ref name="live-20170423"/> It was the first time since [[2002 French presidential election|2002]] that a National Front candidate continued to the second round and the first time in the history of the Fifth Republic that the runoff did not include a nominee of the traditional left or right parties;<ref>{{cite news|title=Macron et Le Pen au second tour d'une présidentielle hors norme|url=http://www.sudouest.fr/2017/04/23/macron-et-le-pen-au-second-tour-d-une-presidentielle-hors-norme-3389901-6121.php|agency=Agence France-Presse|newspaper=Sud-Ouest|date=23 April 2017|access-date=23 April 2017|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170424090214/http://www.sudouest.fr/2017/04/23/macron-et-le-pen-au-second-tour-d-une-presidentielle-hors-norme-3389901-6121.php|archive-date=24 April 2017}}</ref> their combined share of the vote from eligible voters, at approximately 26%, was also a historic low.<ref>{{cite news|author=Maxime Vaudano|title=Présidentielle 2017 : un revers inédit dans la Ve République pour les deux grands partis français|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/les-decodeurs/article/2017/04/23/presidentielle-2017-un-revers-inedit-dans-la-ve-republique-pour-les-deux-grands-partis-francais_5115977_4355770.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=23 April 2017|access-date=23 April 2017|archive-date=6 July 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220706082607/https://www.lemonde.fr/les-decodeurs/article/2017/04/23/presidentielle-2017-un-revers-inedit-dans-la-ve-republique-pour-les-deux-grands-partis-francais_5115977_4355770.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Estimations of the result of the second round on 7 May indicated that Macron had been elected by a decisive margin; Le Pen immediately conceded defeat.<ref name="live-20170507"/> After the [[Minister of the Interior (France)|Interior Ministry]] published preliminary results, the official result of the second round was proclaimed by the [[Constitutional Council (France)|Constitutional Council]] on 10 May. Overall, 43.6% of the registered electorate voted for Macron; in 2002, by contrast, two-thirds of eligible voters voted against then-FN candidate [[Jean-Marie Le Pen]].<ref>{{cite news|author1=Maxime Vaudano|author2=Adrien Sénécat|author3=Eléa Pommiers|title=Le second tour de la présidentielle 2017 en 8 chiffres|url=https://www.lemonde.fr/les-decodeurs/article/2017/05/07/le-second-tour-de-la-presidentielle-2017-en-8-chiffres_5123931_4355770.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=7 May 2017|access-date=18 December 2018|archive-date=19 December 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181219134317/https://www.lemonde.fr/les-decodeurs/article/2017/05/07/le-second-tour-de-la-presidentielle-2017-en-8-chiffres_5123931_4355770.html|url-status=live}}</ref> When Macron took office on 14 May, he became the youngest holder of the presidency in French history and the youngest French head of state since [[Napoleon]]. He named [[Édouard Philippe]] as [[Prime Minister of France|Prime Minister]] the next day. The [[First Philippe government|initial government]] was assembled on 17 May; [[2017 French legislative election|legislative elections]] on 11 and 18 June gave En Marche! a substantial majority.

== Background ==
The [[President of France|President of the French Republic]] and [[Co-Prince of Andorra|French Co-Prince of Andorra]] is elected to a five-year term in a two-round election under Article 7 of the [[Constitution of France|Constitution]]: if no candidate secures an absolute majority of votes in the first round, a second round is held two weeks later between the two candidates who received the most votes.<ref>{{cite web|title=Constitution du 4 octobre 1958 - Article 7|url=https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/affichTexteArticle.do?idArticle=LEGIARTI000006527463&cidTexte=LEGITEXT000006071194&dateTexte=19621108|publisher=Légifrance|access-date=22 February 2017|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170223125733/https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/affichTexteArticle.do?idArticle=LEGIARTI000006527463&cidTexte=LEGITEXT000006071194&dateTexte=19621108|archive-date=23 February 2017}}</ref> In 2017, the first and second rounds were held 23 April and 7 May.<ref name="rules">{{cite news|author=Patrick Roger|title=Parrainages, temps de parole, débats… Les dates clé de l'élection présidentielle|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/02/15/parrainages-temps-de-parole-debats-les-dates-cle-de-l-election-presidentielle_5079909_4854003.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=15 February 2017|access-date=22 February 2017|archive-date=5 May 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200505144301/https://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/02/15/parrainages-temps-de-parole-debats-les-dates-cle-de-l-election-presidentielle_5079909_4854003.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Each presidential candidate must meet a specific set of [[List of presidential qualifications by country|requirements]] in order to run. They must be a French citizen of at least 18 years old. It is also necessary for candidates to be on an [[electoral roll]], proving their eligibility to vote. 

To be listed on the first-round ballot, candidates must secure 500 signatures<ref>{{Citation|title=Décision n° 81-30 ORGA du Conseil constitutionnel du 24 février 1981|url=https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/affichTexte.do?cidTexte=JORFTEXT000000879634|access-date=2020-01-02|archive-date=16 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220516152759/https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/affichTexte.do?cidTexte=JORFTEXT000000879634|url-status=live}}</ref> (often referred to as ''parrainages'') from national or local elected officials from at least 30 different [[departments of France|departments]] or [[overseas collectivity|overseas collectivities]], with no more than a tenth of these signatories from any single department.<ref name="parrainages">{{cite web|title=Concernant les parrainages, qu'est-ce qui a changé depuis 2012 ?|url=http://presidentielle2017.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/tout-savoir/parrainages/concernant-parrainages-quest-a-change-2012/|publisher=Conseil constitutionnel présidentielle 2017|access-date=12 February 2017|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170203064440/http://presidentielle2017.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/tout-savoir/parrainages/concernant-parrainages-quest-a-change-2012/|archive-date= 3 February 2017}}</ref> The official signature collection period followed the publication of the [[Journal officiel de la République française|''Journal officiel'']] on 25 February to 17 March.<ref name="dates">{{cite news|author=Patrick Roger|title=Présidentielle : que prévoit la Constitution en cas de retrait de la candidature de Fillon ?|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/02/10/les-dates-cles-d-ici-a-l-election-presidentielle_5077684_4854003.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=10 February 2017|access-date=22 February 2017|archive-date=5 May 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200505144309/https://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/02/10/les-dates-cles-d-ici-a-l-election-presidentielle_5077684_4854003.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The collection period had initially been scheduled to begin on 23 February, but a visit by Prime Minister [[Bernard Cazeneuve]] to China on that date forced a delay.<ref>{{cite news|author=Sébastien Tronche|title=Présidentielle : pourquoi la période d'ouverture des parrainages est reportée ? (spoiler : c'est la faute de Cazeneuve)|url=http://lelab.europe1.fr/presidentielle-pourquoi-la-periode-douverture-des-parrainages-est-reportee-spoiler-cest-la-faute-de-cazeneuve-2983682|work=Le Lab|publisher=Europe 1|date=21 February 2017|access-date=22 February 2017|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170223130408/http://lelab.europe1.fr/presidentielle-pourquoi-la-periode-douverture-des-parrainages-est-reportee-spoiler-cest-la-faute-de-cazeneuve-2983682|archive-date=23 February 2017}}</ref> French prefectures mailed sponsorship forms to the 42,000 elected officials eligible to give their signature to a candidate, which must then be delivered to the [[Constitutional Council (France)|Constitutional Council]] for validation. Unlike in previous years, a list of validated signatures was posted on Tuesday and Thursday of every week on the council's website; in the past, signatories were published only after the official candidate list had been verified after the end of the collection period. The end of the signature collection period also marked the deadline for the declaration of personal assets required of prospective candidates. The final list of candidates was declared on 21 March.<ref name="dates" />{{bar box
|float=right
|title=Speaking time of candidates and supporters from 1&nbsp;February to 10&nbsp;April recorded by the [[Conseil supérieur de l'audiovisuel|CSA]]<ref>{{cite news|author1=Gary Dagorn|author2=Agathe Dahyot|author3=Maxime Vaudano|title=Sondages, temps de parole... suivez la campagne présidentielle avec quatre indicateurs|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/les-decodeurs/visuel/2017/03/07/sondages-parrainages-meetings-temps-de-parole-quatre-indicateurs-pour-suivre-la-campagne-presidentielle_5090550_4355770.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=7 March 2017|access-date=14 April 2017|archive-date=26 April 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170426183716/http://www.lemonde.fr/les-decodeurs/visuel/2017/03/07/sondages-parrainages-meetings-temps-de-parole-quatre-indicateurs-pour-suivre-la-campagne-presidentielle_5090550_4355770.html|url-status=live}}</ref>
