| A former member of the [[Rally for the Republic|RPR]], [[Rally for France|RPF]] and [[Union for a Popular Movement|UMP]], Dupont-Aignan left the latter party on the eve of the [[2007 French presidential election|2007 presidential election]] due to disagreements with [[Nicolas Sarkozy]]. He subsequently founded the [[souverainism|sovereignist]] political party [[Debout la République]] (DLR), which was later renamed [[Debout la France]] (DLF) in 2014. He previously stood as a candidate in the [[2012 French presidential election|2012 presidential election]], in which he garnered 1.79% of the vote in the first round. Claiming the mantle of [[Gaullism]], he sought to position himself between [[Marine Le Pen|Le Pen]] and [[François Fillon|Fillon]].<ref name="candidates">{{cite news|title=Qui sont les candidats pour la présidentielle 2017 ?|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/03/18/deux-femmes-neuf-hommes-qui-sont-les-candidats-qualifies-pour-l-election-presidentielle_5096791_4854003.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=18 March 2017|access-date=19 March 2017|archive-date=19 March 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170319060108/http://www.lemonde.fr//election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/03/18/deux-femmes-neuf-hommes-qui-sont-les-candidats-qualifies-pour-l-election-presidentielle_5096791_4854003.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Five days after his elimination in the first round, he announced his support for Le Pen in the second round.<ref name="NDA-MLP">{{cite news|title=Nicolas Dupont-Aignan soutient Marine Le Pen pour le second tour|url=http://www.bfmtv.com/politique/nicolas-dupont-aignan-a-annonce-son-soutien-a-marine-le-pen-1153182.html|publisher=BFM TV|date=28 April 2017|access-date=28 April 2017|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170428224852/http://www.bfmtv.com/politique/nicolas-dupont-aignan-a-annonce-son-soutien-a-marine-le-pen-1153182.html|archive-date=28 April 2017}}</ref>
