When Le Pen, a lawyer by occupation, stood in the [[2012 French presidential election|2012 presidential election]], she came in third with 17.90% of first-round votes. She rose within the ranks of the [[National Rally (France)|National Front]] (FN), founded and previously led by her father [[Jean-Marie Le Pen]], culminating in a bitter leadership struggle which she won in 2011. Her campaign programme prioritised the national interests of France and an exit from the [[eurozone]],<ref name="candidates" /> emphasising her party's traditional concern about security and immigration, as well as socioeconomic issues and the sovereignty of the French state, on matters of currency, borders, the economy and rule of law.<ref name="candidates-figaro">{{cite news|title=Présidentielle : qui sont les 11 candidats sur la ligne de départ|url=http://www.lefigaro.fr/elections/presidentielles/2017/03/18/35003-20170318ARTFIG00052-presidentielle-qui-sont-les-11-candidats-sur-la-ligne-de-depart.php|newspaper=Le Figaro|date=18 March 2017|access-date=19 March 2017|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170318141422/http://www.lefigaro.fr/elections/presidentielles/2017/03/18/35003-20170318ARTFIG00052-presidentielle-qui-sont-les-11-candidats-sur-la-ligne-de-depart.php|archive-date=18 March 2017}}</ref> Her campaign was punctuated by judicial inquiries into her party and personal associates.<ref name="candidates" />
