| '''Spokeswoman of [[Lutte Ouvrière]]'''<br /><small>(since 2008)</small>
|
| Arthaud first ran for the presidency in the [[2012 French presidential election|2012 election]] under the LO banner, receiving 0.56% of votes in the first round. A professor of economics, she described the objective of her candidacy as being to, "make the workers' voice heard", hoping to "allow workers, the unemployed and exploited to defend their interests, as opposed to [those who pocketed] millions and millions".<ref name="candidates"/> She claims that she is the only communist candidate, and wants to see borders disappear and overthrow capitalism.<ref name="candidates-figaro"/> She intended to cast a [[protest vote|blank vote]] in the second round.<ref name="live-20170423"/>
|-
| style="background:lavender;"| '''[[Philippe Poutou]]''' ({{Age|1967|3|14|2017|3|21}})<br />[[New Anticapitalist Party]] (NPA)
| [[File:Les Gilets Jaunes (33249708098) (cropped).jpg|80px|Philippe Poutou]]
| style="background-color:{{party color|New Anticapitalist Party}};" |
| '''Spokesperson of the [[New Anticapitalist Party]]'''<br /><small>(since 2009)</small>
|
| A long-time radical left-wing activist, as well as a trade unionist and Ford mechanic in [[Blanquefort, Gironde|Blanquefort]], Poutou led opposition to the shutdown of the local factory. He also ran in the [[2012 French presidential election|2012 presidential election]], obtaining 1.15% of votes. He launched his political activities at [[Lutte Ouvrière]] before joining the [[Revolutionary Communist League (France)|Revolutionary Communist League]] (LCR) which became the NPA in 2009.<ref name="candidates"/> With Marxist and anarchist roots, he crusades against capitalism and espouses radical-left ideas.<ref name="candidates-figaro"/> He offered no voting instructions to his supporters for the second round.<ref name="live-20170423"/>
|-
| style="background:lavender;"| '''[[Jacques Cheminade]]''' ({{Age|1941|8|20|2017|3|21}})<br />[[Solidarity and Progress]] (S&P)
| [[File:Jacques cheminade 22102209 (cropped).jpg|80px|Jacques Cheminade]]
| style="background-color:{{party color|Solidarity and Progress}};" |
| '''President of [[Solidarity and Progress]]'''<br /><small>(since 1996)</small>
| [[File:Logo candidature Cheminade 2017.svg|140px|Logo of Jacques Cheminade]]
| Cheminade founded [[Solidarity and Progress]] in 1996 and is the figurehead of the [[LaRouche movement]] in France. He proposes leaving [[NATO]], the [[European Union|EU]], the [[eurozone]] and returning to the [[French franc|franc]]. He supports [[Colonization of the Moon|colonisation of the Moon]] to facilitate [[exploration of Mars]]. He was a candidate twice before, in [[1995 French presidential election|1995]] and [[2012 French presidential election|2012]], collecting 0.28% and 0.25% of the vote, respectively, but failed to appear on the ballot in 1981, 1988, 2002 and 2007.<ref name="candidates"/> His position on the second round is unclear,<ref name="live-20170423"/> only specifying that he, personally, would not cast a vote for Le Pen while also denouncing the forces of "financial occupation".<ref name="Cheminade">{{cite news|title=Fillon, Mélenchon, Hamon, Poutou... Quelle est la consigne de vote des neuf éliminés en vue du second tour ?|url=http://www.francetvinfo.fr/elections/presidentielle/fillon-melenchon-hamon-poutou-quelle-est-la-consigne-de-vote-des-neuf-elimines-en-vue-du-second-tour_2158950.html|publisher=franceinfo|date=24 April 2017|access-date=7 May 2017|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170428211325/http://www.francetvinfo.fr/elections/presidentielle/fillon-melenchon-hamon-poutou-quelle-est-la-consigne-de-vote-des-neuf-elimines-en-vue-du-second-tour_2158950.html|archive-date=28 April 2017}}</ref>
|-
| style="background:lavender;"| '''[[Jean Lassalle]]''' ({{Age|1955|5|3|2017|3|21}})<br />Résistons!
| [[File:Jean Lassalle 05 (cropped).jpg|80px|Jean Lassalle]]
| style="background:{{party color|Miscellaneous right}};"|
| '''[[Deputies of the 14th National Assembly of France by constituency|Deputy]] for [[Pyrénées-Atlantiques]]'''<br /><small>(since 2002)</small><br />'''[[Mayor (France)|Mayor]] of [[Lourdios-Ichère]]'''<br /><small>(since 1977)</small>
| [[File:Jean Lassalle 2017 logo.png|140px|Logo of Jean Lassalle]]
| Lassalle, a former member of the [[Democratic Movement (France)|Democratic Movement]] (MoDem) and associate of [[François Bayrou]] running under the banner of Résistons!, considered himself the "defender of rural territories and a humanist ecology". He became famous for a successful 39-day [[hunger strike]] protesting the movement of the [[Total S.A.|Total]] factory from [[Accous]] to the [[Lacq gas field|Lacq basin]] {{convert|65|km|mi|abbr=on}} away. In 2013, he walked {{convert|6000|km|mi|abbr=on}} on foot to "meet the French".<ref name="candidates"/> He opted to cast a [[protest vote|blank vote]] in the second round.<ref name="Lassalle-blanc"/>
|-
| style="background:lavender;"| '''[[Jean-Luc Mélenchon]]''' ({{Age|1951|8|19|2017|3|21}})<br />[[La France Insoumise]] (FI)
| [[File:Jean-Luc Mélenchon (9301728809) (cropped).jpg|80px|Jean-Luc Mélenchon]]
| style="background-color:{{party color|La France Insoumise}};" |
| '''[[Member of the European Parliament|MEP]] for [[South-West France (European Parliament constituency)|South-West France]]'''<br /><small>(2009–2017)</small>
{{Hidden|Other offices|
* President, then co-president of the [[Left Party (France)|Left Party]] from 2009 to 2014
* [[Senate (France)|Senator]] for [[Essonne]] from 1986 to 2000 and from 2004 to 2010
* [[Ministry of National Education (France)|Deputy Minister of Vocational Education]] from 2000 to 2002}}
| [[File:Jean-Luc Mélenchon 2017 logo.png|140px|Logo of Jean-Luc Mélenchon]]
| Denouncing the "liberal drift" of the party, Mélenchon left the [[Socialist Party (France)|PS]] in 2008 to found the [[Left Party (France)|Left Party]]. He made a previous presidential run in [[2012 French presidential election|2012]], coming in fourth with 11.10% of votes,<ref name="candidates"/> with the backing of the [[French Communist Party]] (PCF). A critic of the [[presidency of François Hollande]], he launched his 2017 bid without consulting the PCF, instead choosing to start his own movement, [[La France Insoumise]] (FI).<ref name="candidates-figaro"/> He later won the PCF's support by a narrow margin.<ref>{{cite news|title=Les militants communistes votent à 53,6% en faveur d'un soutien à Jean-Luc Mélenchon pour l'élection présidentielle|url=http://www.francetvinfo.fr/politique/melenchon/les-militants-communistes-votent-a-53-6-en-faveur-d-un-soutien-a-jean-luc-melenchon-pour-l-election-presidentielle_1940593.html|agency=Agence France-Presse; Reuters|publisher=franceinfo|date=26 November 2016|access-date=17 April 2017|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161219004435/http://www.francetvinfo.fr/politique/melenchon/les-militants-communistes-votent-a-53-6-en-faveur-d-un-soutien-a-jean-luc-melenchon-pour-l-election-presidentielle_1940593.html|archive-date=19 December 2016}}</ref> His programme underlined left-wing and environmental principles,<ref name="candidates"/> including the establishment of a [[French Sixth Republic|Sixth Republic]], redistribution of wealth, renegotiating [[European Union|EU]] treaties, environmental planning and protecting the independence of France, namely from the [[United States]]. He sought a withdrawal from NATO and was largely criticised for refusing to denounce authoritarian leaders, most notably [[Nicolas Maduro]].<ref name="candidates-figaro"/> He ran an innovative campaign, gathering a large following on social media<ref>{{cite news|author=Marc de Boni|title=Mélenchon poursuit sa stratégie gagnante sur YouTube|url=http://www.lefigaro.fr/elections/presidentielles/2017/01/09/35003-20170109ARTFIG00246-melenchon-poursuit-sa-strategie-gagnante-sur-youtube.php|newspaper=Le Figaro|date=9 January 2017|access-date=17 April 2017|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170409073634/http://www.lefigaro.fr/elections/presidentielles/2017/01/09/35003-20170109ARTFIG00246-melenchon-poursuit-sa-strategie-gagnante-sur-youtube.php|archive-date= 9 April 2017}}</ref> and holding simultaneous meetings in multiple cities via hologram.<ref>{{cite news|author=Florian Maussion|title=VIDEO. Hologramme : et soudain Jean-Luc Mélenchon se dédoubla...|url=http://www.leparisien.fr/elections/presidentielle/l-hologramme-de-jean-luc-melenchon-en-meeting-comment-ca-marche-05-02-2017-6656853.php|newspaper=Le Parisien|date=5 February 2017|access-date=17 April 2017|archive-date=18 April 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170418081159/http://www.leparisien.fr/elections/presidentielle/l-hologramme-de-jean-luc-melenchon-en-meeting-comment-ca-marche-05-02-2017-6656853.php|url-status=live}}</ref> He intended to consult with his movement before making any pronouncement on the second round.<ref name="live-20170423"/> After a few days, he stated that he would not vote for the FN, but never explicitly provided any further voting instructions.<ref>{{cite news|title=Jean-Luc Mélenchon " ne votera pas FN " mais ne donne pas de consigne de vote|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/04/28/jean-luc-melenchon-ne-votera-pas-front-national-mais-ne-donne-pas-de-consigne-de-vote_5119675_4854003.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=28 April 2017|access-date=15 May 2017|archive-date=28 April 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170428230632/http://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/04/28/jean-luc-melenchon-ne-votera-pas-front-national-mais-ne-donne-pas-de-consigne-de-vote_5119675_4854003.html|url-status=live}}</ref>
|-
| style="background:lavender;"| '''[[François Asselineau]]''' ({{Age|1957|9|14|2017|3|21}})<br />[[Popular Republican Union (2007)|Popular Republican Union]] (UPR)
| [[File:François ASSELINEAU.jpg|80px|François Asselineau]]
| style="background-color:{{party color|Popular Republican Union}};" |
| '''President of the [[Popular Republican Union (2007)|Popular Republican Union]]'''<br /><small>(since 2007)</small>
| [[File:François Asselineau 2017 logo.png|140px|Logo of François Asselineau]]
| A [[souverainism|sovereignist]], Asselineau surprised political observers with his ability to secure the 500 sponsorships required to stand as a candidate. Formerly of the [[Rally for France|RPF]] and [[Union for a Popular Movement|UMP]], he founded the [[Popular Republican Union (2007)|Popular Republican Union]] (UPR) in 2007 and has agitated for the French to exit from the [[European Union|EU]].<ref name="candidates"/> Sometimes classified as a far-right Eurosceptic, he has denounced "American imperialism" and proposed leaving [[NATO]].<ref name="candidates-figaro"/> He offered no endorsement in the second round.<ref name="live-20170423"/>
|-
| style="background:lavender;"| '''[[François Fillon]]''' ({{Age|1954|3|4|2017|3|21}})<br />[[The Republicans (France)|The Republicans]] (LR)
| [[File:François Fillon 2010 (cropped).jpg|80px|François Fillon]]
| style="background-color:{{party color|The Republicans (France)}};" |
| '''[[Deputies of the 14th National Assembly of France by constituency|Deputy]] for [[Paris]]'''<br /><small>(2012–2017)</small><br />'''[[Prime Minister of France|Prime Minister]]'''<br /><small>(2007–2012)</small>
{{Hidden|Other offices|
* Co-president of the [[Union for a Popular Movement]] ''(interim)'' in 2014
* President of the [[Rassemblement-UMP|Rassemblement-UMP group]] in the [[National Assembly (France)|National Assembly]] from 2012 to 2013
* [[Ministry of Ecology, Sustainable Development and Energy|Minister of Ecology, Sustainable Development, Transport]] and [[Minister of Housing and Territorial Equality (France)|Housing]] in 2012
* [[National Assembly (France)|Deputy]] for [[Sarthe]] from 1981 to 1993, from 1997 to 2002 and in 2007
* [[Senate (France)|Senator]] for the [[Sarthe]] in 2004 and from 2005 to 2007
* [[Ministry of National Education (France)|Minister of National Education, Higher Education and Research]] from 2004 to 2005
* [[Minister of Social Affairs (France)|Minister of Social Affairs, Labour and Solidarity]] from 2002 to 2004
* [[President of the Regional Council (France)|President]] of the [[Regional council (France)|regional council]] of [[Pays de la Loire]] from 1998 to 2002
* [[Mayor (France)|Mayor]] of [[Sablé-sur-Sarthe]] from 1983 to 2001
* [[President of the General Council|President]] of the [[Departmental councils (France)|general council]] of the [[Sarthe]] from 1992 to 1998
* Deputy Minister responsible for [[Minister of Posts, Telegraphs, and Telephones|Post, Telecommunications and Space]] from 1995 to 1997
* [[Minister of Posts, Telegraphs, and Telephones|Minister of Information Technology and Post]] in 1995
* [[Ministry of National Education (France)|Minister of Higher Education and Research]] from 1993 to 1995}}
| [[File:F 2017.svg|80px|Logo of François Fillon]]
| Fillon led a prolific political career starting from the early 1970s. The surprise winner of the [[2016 The Republicans (France) presidential primary|primary of the right]] offered a liberal economic program ending the [[35-hour workweek]], dismissing 500,000 civil servants, abolishing the [[solidarity tax on wealth|wealth tax]] (ISF), streamlining the labour code, and reforming the [[Health care in France#Health insurance|health insurance system]]. However, his campaign was hobbled in January 2017 following the publication of allegations of fictitious employment of family members, including his wife, collectively known as "[[Fillon affair|Penelopegate]]". He initially said he would drop his bid if placed under formal investigation, but continued his candidacy after such investigations began on 15 March.<ref name="candidates"/> He endorsed [[Emmanuel Macron]] in the second round.<ref name="live-20170423"/>
|}

=== Sponsorships ===
A candidate must secure 500 signatures from elected officials in order to appear on the first-round ballot,<ref name="parrainages"/> with the signature collection period ending on 17 March.<ref name="dates"/> The table below lists sponsorships received by the Constitutional Council by candidate.<ref name="parrainages-par-candidat">{{cite web|title=Les parrainages par candidat|url=https://presidentielle2017.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/les-parrainages/parrainages-par-candidat/|publisher=Conseil constitutionnel présidentielle 2017|date=18 March 2017|access-date=18 March 2017|archive-date=19 September 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170919161008/https://presidentielle2017.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/les-parrainages/parrainages-par-candidat/|url-status=live}}</ref>

